<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kodagu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kodagu, formerly Coorg, is a hilly district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, where the Kaveri river rises. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madikeri">Madikeri</a>. It was ruled by the Haleri rajas from about 1600 until British annexation in 1834, remained a separate province until 1956, and is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodava-people">Kodava people</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kodagu.jpg" alt="Kodagu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jyotirmoy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tadiandamol_Valley,_Western_Ghats.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
